e covered; no orphaned requirements and no bundled HLRs.\n64\t\n65\t## Health Warnings\n66\t\n67\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded with inferred teams and acceptance criteria derived from the HLRs.\n68\t- **Inferred team ownership**: the architecture context (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`) assigns KServe to RHOAI Serving; the Platform Operator owning team is not stated anywhere in the strategy or context — E002 is assigned to \"RHOAI Platform\" as an assumption that should be verified at planning.\n69\t- **Platform Operator lacks an architecture-context file**: only `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` exists. Platform Operator is treated as an existing in-platform component (it is in the canonical RHAI component list and the strategy presupposes it is deployed today), so no onboarding chain was generated — but its `architecture_claims` signal is scored 0 (no corroboration available), and if its image/repo surface differs from what the strategy assumes, E002's scope needs revisit.\n70\t- **Scope ambiguity — \"serving data plane\"**: the problem statement says the serving data plane links non-validated crypto, but the Affected Components list names only KServe and Platform Operator. Per `kserve.md`, model execution in the data plane is provided by Serving Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit), which are not listed as affected. E001 covers the data plane images under the KServe epic; **re-split trigger**: if the module inventory finds data-plane images owned by a Serving Runtimes team or more than ~3 distinct image groups requiring different remediation approaches, E001 must be re-split along component/owning-team lines and a new epic added for Serving Runtimes.\n71\t- **Epic size overage (E001)**: remediating all KServe component and data plane images in one epic may exceed the ~2-week guideline. It is kept as one epic because it is one logical change — a single mechanical crypto-remediation pass producing one module inventory; splitting would duplicate the audit. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along image-group/sub-system lines if the module inventory finds materially more images than expected (more than ~3 distinct image groups) or work spanning multiple owning teams.\n72\t- **External schedule risk (not an AC)**: certification lab turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. These are recorded as risks on E003; its acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion point we control (complete, reviewed, submitted package).\n73\t- **Docs priority note**: E005 carries P0 despite HLR-4 being P2 — this is the mandated docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority is Critical), not a priority inversion. It does not push priority onto its dependencies.\n74\t\n75\t## Tiered Delivery\n76\t\n77\tNot applicable. The external dependency (accredited lab certification) does not gate any engineering deliverable: remediation (E001/E002), CI enforcement (E004), and documentation (E005) all deliver value independent of lab timing. The only lab-gated outcome is the certification decision itself, which is outside the strategy's engineering scope; E003's controllable deliverable is the submitted evidence package.\n78\t\n79\t## Component Mappings\n80\t\n81\t- E003 (evidence package) spans all serving-stack images (KServe, data plane, Platform Operator); assigned canonical component `KServe` as the single most-relevant name for the serving stack.\n82\t- E004 (CI regression gate) similarly spans serving-stack builds including Platform Operator; assigned canonical component `KServe`.\n83\t- E005 is assigned canonical component `Documentation` (docs content work).\n84\t- All component values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n85\t