d against non-remediated images. |\n45\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (artifact consumer) | Same as above for the Platform Operator images the evidence package must cover. |\n46\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the implemented FIPS posture; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n47\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n48\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n49\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n50\t| (no edge) E001/E002 ↔ E004 | Rule 14/15 (parallel) | The CI regression check is a different work-product type in a different area (pipeline config vs image content). Its coupling to the validated-module inventory is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E004, not a DAG edge. |\n51\t| (no edge) E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different components and repos, no shared artifacts — remediations proceed independently. |\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic(s) | Notes |\n56\t|-----|----------|------------------------|---------|-------|\n57\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules | E001, E002 | Split by component/team boundary (KServe + data plane vs Platform Operator), not by priority. |\n58\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through security review board to external lab | E003 | |\n59\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI gating check fails builds reintroducing non-validated crypto | E004 | Distinct, deferrable feature (CI enforcement) with independent value; does not share implementation surface with the P0 image remediation — separately schedulable, so priority-split per Step 4. |\n60\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | E005 | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy frontmatter (Critical → P0) per the docs-authoring priority exception, not from HLR-4's P2 label. |\n61\t\n62\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements and no bundled HLRs.\n63\t\n64\t## Health Warnings\n65\t\n66\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded with inferred teams and acceptance criteria derived from the HLRs.\n67\t- **Inferred team ownership**: the architecture context (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`) assigns KServe to RHOAI Serving; the Platform Operator owning team is not stated anywhere in the strategy or context — E002 is assigned to \"RHOAI Platform\" as an assumption that should be verified at planning.\n68\t- **Platform Operator lacks an architecture-context file**: only `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` exists. Platform Operator is treated as an existing in-platform component (it is in the canonical RHAI component list and the strategy presupposes it is deployed today), so no onboarding chain was generated — but its `architecture_claims` signal is scored 0 (no corroboration available), and if its image/repo surface differs from what the strategy assumes, E002's scope needs revisit.\n69\t- **Scope ambiguity — \"serving data plane\"**: the problem statement says the serving data plane links non-validated crypto, but the Affected Components list names only KServe and Platform Operator. Per `kserve.md`, model execution in the data plane is provided by Serving Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit), which are not listed as affected. E001 covers the data plane images under the KServe epic; **re-split trigger**: if the module inventory finds data-plane images owned by a Serving Runtimes team or more than ~3 distinct image groups requiring different remediation approaches, E001 must be re-split along component/owning-team lines and a new epic added for Serving Runtimes.\n70\t- **Epic size overage (E001)**: remediating all KServe component and data plane images in one epic may exceed the ~2-week guideline. It is kept as one epic because it is one logical change — a single mechanical crypto-remediation pass producing one module inventory; splitting would duplicate the audit. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along image-group/sub-system lines if the module inventory finds materially more images than expected (more than ~3 distinct image groups) or work spanning multiple owning teams.\n71\t- **External schedule risk (not an AC)**: certification lab turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. These are recorded as risks on E003; its acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion point we control (complete, reviewed, submitted package).\n72\t- **Docs priority note**: E005 carries P0 despite HLR-4 being P2 — this is the mandated docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority is Critical), not a priority inversion. It does not push priority onto its dependencies.\n73\t\n74\t## Tiered Delivery\n75\t\n76\tNot applicable. The external dependency (accredited lab certification) does not gate any engineering deliverable: remediation (E001/E002), CI enforcement (E004), and documentation (E005) all deliver value independent of lab timing. The only lab-gated outcome is the certification decision itself, which is outside the strategy's engineering scope; E003's controllable deliverable is the submitted evidence package.\n77\t\n78\t## Component Mappings\n79\t\n80\t- E003 (evidence package) spans all serving-stack images (KServe, data plane, Platform Operator); assigned canonical component `KServe` as the single most-relevant name for the serving stack.\n81\t- E004 (CI regression gate) similarly spans serving-stack builds including Platform Operator; assigned canonical component `KServe`.\n82\t- E005 is assigned canonical component `Documentation` (docs content work).\n83\t- All component values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_us